---
// Help: submitting a paper (docs/CONTRIBUTIONS.md, "Submit"; src/pages/submit.astro;
// website/worker/contributions/; oscr/jobs.py).
import Info from "../../components/Info.astro";
import { SITE_NAME } from "../../config";

const toc = [
  { id: "when", text: "When to submit" },
  { id: "steps", text: "Step by step" },
  { id: "checks", text: "The checks made at once" },
  { id: "draft", text: "The draft, and publishing it" },
  { id: "refused", text: "Why a submission may be refused" },
  { id: "limits", text: "Limits" },
];
---
<Info
  title="Submitting a paper"
  description={`How to submit a paper and its authors' code to ${SITE_NAME}: the checks, the draft, and publication.`}
  group="help"
  path="/help/submit/"
  parents={[{ text: "Help", href: "/help/" }]}
  toc={toc}
>
  <p class="lead" slot="lead">
    {SITE_NAME} finds most papers by itself. When it has missed one — or missed part of its code — anyone signed in can
    submit it: a DOI and the links to the authors' code. The registry then treats it as it treats every paper.
  </p>

  <h2 id="when">When to submit</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A neuroscience paper whose authors published their code, and that the <a href="/lookup/">DOI lookup</a> does not
      know, or knows without its code.</li>
    <li>A paper whose code {SITE_NAME} found only in part. (If you are an author or a maintainer of its code, you can also
      <a href="/help/correct/">correct its record</a> directly.)</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="steps">Step by step</h2>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><a href="/account/">Sign in</a> — with ORCID if you are an author: you are then recognized at once.</li>
    <li>On <a href="/submit/">the submission page</a>, give the paper's DOI and one to five links to its authors' code,
      one a line, and, if you wish, a note for the moderators (without an email address: any address is removed).</li>
    <li>The site checks the DOI and the links at once and records your submission, or tells you in words what is wrong.</li>
    <li>{SITE_NAME}'s harvester reads the paper as it reads every paper, verifies each link at its source — its commit,
      its licence, its scripts — and matches the code with the paper's paragraphs.</li>
    <li>A <strong>draft</strong> of the record comes back to your account page. Review it; correct the links if need be
      (the harvester reads them again); then publish.</li>
    <li>When your ORCID iD is among the paper's authors, the record is published at once. Otherwise the operator looks at
      it first. Either way, the next nightly publication brings it to the site.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="checks">The checks made at once</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The DOI is registered</strong>: asked of doi.org's own index.</li>
    <li><strong>Each link points to a place the registry knows</strong>: a forge (GitHub, GitLab, Codeberg, Bitbucket,
      GIN…), an archive (Zenodo, OSF, figshare, Software Heritage, Code Ocean, Hugging Face…) or a data repository — the
      same rule as the harvester's. Any other address is refused, and the site fetches nothing else.</li>
    <li><strong>Each link answers</strong>: a link that answers "not found" or "gone" refuses the submission; a slow or
      failing server is left to the harvester to judge.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The licence is not checked at once: reading it needs the repository itself, which the harvester does.</p>

  <h2 id="draft">The draft, and publishing it</h2>
  <p>
    The draft is the record exactly as it would be published: the paper's metadata, each repository with its state,
    licence and commit, and the number of matches. You may correct its links up to ten times. Published, your links
    become corrections of the record — a new version, which the Versions section records as a correction, without your
    name. A draft's matches need the paper's full text from Europe PMC; without it, they come after publication.
  </p>

  <h2 id="refused">Why a submission may be refused</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>the DOI is not registered, or a link is dead;</li>
    <li>a link points to a place the registry does not know;</li>
    <li>you already submitted this DOI (one submission per account and paper);</li>
    <li>the paper is not neuroscience: it stays out of {SITE_NAME}, whatever its code (<a href="/taxonomy/#scope">the scope</a>);</li>
    <li>the operator, reading a submission by someone who is not among the authors, finds it wrong — and says why, in
      words you read on your account page.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="limits">Limits</h2>
  <p>At most 10 submissions a day per account, 1 to 5 code links each, a note of 1,000 characters at most.</p>
</Info>
